he three pathogens were also evaluated using pathogen-inoculated semen samples and were as few as 50 Leptospira organisms, 50 CFU Brucella, and 1 TCID_{50} BoHV-1. Semen samples from elite bulls at a germplasm center were also tested in double-blind tests, as a demonstrator for a low-cost, user-friendly point-of-care sensing platform, for in-the-field resource-limited regions. The sensors showed excellent levels of sensitivity and specificity, and for the first time a demonstrated ability of the application of paper microfluidics devices for the diagnosis multiple infectious diseases from semen samples.\n\n## Visual Abstract\n\nImage: Graphic. Refer to the image caption for details.\n\nSubjects Diagnosis, Infectious diseases, Genetics, Immunology, Assays\n\nKeywords nucleic-acid based tests, paper, microfluidics, infectious disease, diagnosis\n\n## Supporting Information\n\nThe Supporting Information is available free of charge on the ACS Publications website at DOI: 10.1021/acssensors.7b00825.\n\n  * Movie describing the processing of samples on the origami paper devices file (AVI)\n\n  * Experimental section; Illustration of the complete sample processing; Agarose gel results of amplification; Optimization of DNA capture area; Real-time LAMP assay; Validation of the paper-origami device (PDF)\n\nRapid Veterinary Diagnosis of Bovine Reproductive Infectious Diseases from Semen Using Paper-Origami DNA Microfluidics\n\n360 views\n\n0 shares\n\n68 downloads\n\nSkip to figshare navigation\n\nS-1\nElectronic Supplementary Information\nRapid Veterinary Diagnosis of Bovine Reproductive Infectious\nDiseases from Semen using Paper-Origami DNA Microfluidics\nZhugen Yang 1, Gaolian Xu 1, Julien Reboud 1, Syed Atif Ali 2, Gurpreet Kaur 2, John\nMcGiven 3, Nongthombam Boby 4, Praveen K Gupta 4, Pallab Chaudhuri 2*, Jonathan M.\nCooper 1*.\n1 Division of Biomedical Engineering, School of Engineer, University of Glasgow, Oakfield Avenue, Glasgow\nG12 8LT, United Kingdom\n2 Divisio